{
  "id": "verben-als-nomen",
  "name": "Gerunds: Swimming is fun",
  "level": "B1",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "subtitle": "A gerund is the -ing form of a verb used like a noun. Use it as the subject (Swimming is fun) and after a preposition (good at cooking, before leaving, interested in learning, look forward to seeing).",
  "briefMarks": [
    "gerund",
    "subject",
    "after a preposition"
  ],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-b1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "verb-ing",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "as the subject"
      },
      {
        "text": "preposition",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "+ verb-ing"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-verb\">Swimming</span> is fun. — I'm good <span class=\"vp-prep\">at</span> cooking. — Call me <span class=\"vp-prep\">before</span> leaving.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>The gerund as a subject.</strong> The -ing form can be the subject of a sentence. It takes a singular verb. <em>Swimming is good for you. Learning English takes time.</em> For a negative, put <em>not</em> before it: <em>Not sleeping makes me tired.</em>",
        "tag": "As a subject"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>After a preposition</strong>, a verb is always in the -ing form. <em>I'm good at cooking. She's interested in learning Italian. Call me before leaving. He left without saying goodbye. Thank you for helping.</em> Never use <em>to + verb</em> or the base verb after a preposition.",
        "tag": "After a preposition"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>look forward to + -ing.</strong> In this phrase, <em>to</em> is a preposition, not the start of an infinitive. So the verb after it ends in -ing: <em>I look forward to seeing you.</em> The same is true for <em>be used to</em>: <em>I'm used to getting up early.</em> A good test: you can say <em>I look forward to it</em> (a noun), so you can say <em>I look forward to seeing you.</em>",
        "tag": "look forward to"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>Swim is fun.</em> → ✓ <em>Swimming is fun.</em> · ✗ <em>I'm interested in to learn Spanish.</em> → ✓ <em>I'm interested in learning Spanish.</em> · ✗ <em>I look forward to see you.</em> → ✓ <em>I look forward to seeing you.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "A gerund as a subject can also be said with <em>it</em> and <em>to</em>: <em>Swimming is fun</em> and <em>It's fun to swim</em> mean the same. The gerund form is common in speech and in writing. After some verbs you choose -ing or <em>to + verb</em>: see <a href=\"/infinitiv-mit-zu\">Verb patterns: -ing or to</a>. For verbs and the prepositions they take (<em>depend on, think about</em>), see <a href=\"/verben-praepositionen\">Verbs + prepositions</a>. For nouns that end in <em>-ing</em> or other endings, see <a href=\"/wortbildung-nomen\">Noun suffixes</a>.",
    "reference": {
